  edit("maclauncher/ADAPTATIONS.md", source => source
    .replace("\nVulkan contract: `VK_KHR_compute_shader_derivatives`, `computeDerivativeGroupLinear`, `computeDerivativeGroupQuads`.\n\nLinear compute groups use four consecutive quad lanes; implicit sampling uses explicit gradients and LOD queries account for sampler bias, mipmap mode and available image levels. Sampled descriptors remain 64 bytes.\n\nLimits and reported properties: `computeDerivativeGroupQuads = false`; `meshAndTaskShaderDerivatives = false`.\n\n",
      () => "\nVulkan contract: `VK_KHR_compute_shader_derivatives`, `computeDerivativeGroupLinear`, `computeDerivativeGroupQuads`.\n\nLinear compute groups use four consecutive quad lanes; implicit sampling uses explicit gradients and LOD queries account for sampler bias, mipmap mode and available image levels. Sampled descriptors remain 64 bytes; sampler bias and mipmap mode come from immutable sampler records. LOD-query probes share one texture-lowering pass.\n\nLimits and reported properties: `computeDerivativeGroupQuads = false`; `meshAndTaskShaderDerivatives = false`.\n\n")
    .replace("\nVulkan contract: Fragment helper semantics and inactive statistics storage.\n\nFragment counters are compiled only for devices enabling pipelineStatisticsQuery, whose state participates in the graphics cache key. Instrumentation uses Metal helper-write suppression after application lowering. Null destinations disable Poly statistics writes, and inactive queries retain ordinary scratch sizes.\n\nScope: fragment helper and demote lowering, inactive tessellation and geometry scratch. Device support and operation restrictions are defined by the linked implementation.\n\n",
      () => "\nVulkan contract: Fragment helper semantics and inactive statistics storage.\n\nFragment counters are compiled only for devices enabling pipelineStatisticsQuery, whose state participates in the graphics cache key. A default-false Metal function constant encloses the complete counter region; active fragment queries select a lazily cached counting specialization. Private query increments use Metal helper-write suppression after application lowering and existing logical-helper predicates in captured bodies. Null destinations disable Poly statistics writes, and inactive queries retain ordinary scratch sizes. Geometry addresses occupy an optional compact per-draw tail.\n\nScope: fragment helper and demote lowering, inactive tessellation and geometry scratch. Device support and operation restrictions are defined by the linked implementation.\n\n")
    .replace("\nHashed locks serialize full-width operations over 32-bit atomic halves. Retry-loop results use a 32-bit pair before reconstruction. Unused native unsigned min/max retain the same lock.\n\nScope: root descriptor upload, device allocation, atomic emission. Device support and operation restrictions are defined by the linked implementation.\n\nConsumers: vkd3d-proton.\n\nImplementation: [kk_physical_device.c](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c), [kk_device.h](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_device.h), [kk_device.c](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_device.c), [kk_cmd_buffer.c](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c), [nir_to_msl.c](../src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c), [kk_atomic_int64.h](../src/kosmickrisp/compiler/kk_atomic_int64.h), [kk_atomic_int64_msl.h](../src/kosmickrisp/compiler/kk_atomic_int64_msl.h).\n\n### Fixed auxiliary words in sampled-image descriptors\n\n",
      () => "\nHashed locks serialize full-width operations over 32-bit atomic halves. Retry-loop results use a 32-bit pair before reconstruction. Unused native unsigned min/max retain the same lock.\n\nThe lock table is created on first atomic function specialization. Its GPU address is a function constant; descriptor roots retain their ordinary address. Shader caches store the requirement and generic source without device addresses.\n\nAtomic helper requirements are collected during the existing type-inference seed visit. Shaders without 64-bit atomic operations emit no lock helpers and require no separate helper-discovery passes.\n\nConsumers: vkd3d-proton.\n\nImplementation: [kk_physical_device.c](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c), [kk_device.h](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_device.h), [kk_device.c](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_device.c), [kk_atomic64.c](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_atomic64.c), [kk_shader.c](../src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_shader.c), [mtl_compiler.m](../src/kosmickrisp/bridge/mtl_compiler.m), [nir_to_msl.c](../src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c), [kk_atomic_int64.h](../src/kosmickrisp/compiler/kk_atomic_int64.h), [kk_atomic_int64_msl.h](../src/kosmickrisp/compiler/kk_atomic_int64_msl.h).\n\n### Fixed auxiliary words in sampled-image descriptors\n\n"));
  edit("maclauncher/reference/KOSMICKRISP_QUERIES.md", source => source
    .replace("\nInput-assembly and vertex statistics use the actual draw arguments, topology and index/restart data. Geometry statistics use the original emitted-primitive counts before transform-feedback capacity limits and polygon expansion. Tessellation statistics use the executed patch and generated domain-point counts. Clipping statistics follow Honeykrisp's implementation-dependent counting model using actual assembled or emitted primitives. They do not measure Metal's internal clipping or replay operations.\n\nDevices created with `pipelineStatisticsQuery` enabled compile fragment entry counters after the application's fragment and helper-invocation lowering. Devices that leave the feature disabled skip this pass and produce fragment shaders without the statistics address check or atomics. The device feature state participates in the [shared shader cache key](KOSMICKRISP_SHADER_CACHE.md), independently of sampler reduction. Metal suppresses atomic writes from helper threads. The counter adds no helper-status or coverage input and preserves the application's demote, derivative and explicit early-test behavior. A pair of 32-bit atomic operations carries into the 64-bit report. Compute statistics use the product of the dispatched workgroup counts and local dimensions. Indirect and predicated dispatches consume the same GPU arguments as the application dispatch. Base-workgroup offsets and internal helper dispatches do not contribute.\n\nThe driver exposes `pipelineStatisticsQuery`. Active queries add the relevant GPU counting helpers, per-draw geometry/tessellation counter storage and ordering barriers. Tessellation uses a zero statistic address without an active TES counter and allocates no statistics scratch. Geometry retains its ordinary 128-byte scratch allocation unless a GS or clipping statistic is active; null statistic addresses skip the Poly writes independently of XFB. The stream parameter layout is unchanged by statistics gating. Fragment shaders contain a uniform counter-address check and perform statistics atomics only while a fragment counter is active. Counting uses the existing queue, compiler, descriptor and query-copy machinery, with no CPU readback during recording or separate GPU queue.\n\nThe adaptation reuses Mesa MIT-licensed Honeykrisp and Poly source, retaining attribution in the owned sources. Timestamps, visibility samples and fabricated zero substitutions do not replace pipeline statistics.\n",
      () => "\nInput-assembly and vertex statistics use the actual draw arguments, topology and index/restart data. Geometry statistics use the original emitted-primitive counts before transform-feedback capacity limits and polygon expansion. Tessellation statistics use the executed patch and generated domain-point counts. Clipping statistics follow Honeykrisp's implementation-dependent counting model using actual assembled or emitted primitives. They do not measure Metal's internal clipping or replay operations.\n\nDevices created with `pipelineStatisticsQuery` enabled compile fragment entry counters after the application's fragment and helper-invocation lowering. Devices that leave the feature disabled skip this pass. A Metal function constant defaults to false and encloses the complete counter region, including its root-address load. An active fragment-invocation query selects a cached counting specialization; its first use compiles that specialization from the existing library. Query-free draws retain the ordinary specialization. Existing framebuffer-read color constants remain in the descriptor chain. Actual application-function metadata controls specialization independently for each native program; capture, replay and support entry points do not inherit application counts. The device feature state and statistics shader ABI participate in the [shared shader cache key](KOSMICKRISP_SHADER_CACHE.md), independently of sampler reduction.\n\nMetal suppresses atomic writes from helper threads. The private query increment adds no helper-status or coverage input and preserves the application's demote, derivative and explicit early-test behavior. Captured application bodies reuse their existing logical-helper predicate. A pair of 32-bit atomic operations carries into the 64-bit report. Compute statistics use the product of the dispatched workgroup counts and local dimensions. Indirect and predicated dispatches consume the same GPU arguments as the application dispatch. Base-workgroup offsets and internal helper dispatches do not contribute.\n\nThe driver exposes `pipelineStatisticsQuery`. Active queries add the relevant GPU counting helpers, per-draw geometry/tessellation counter storage and ordering barriers. Tessellation uses a zero statistic address without an active TES counter and allocates no statistics scratch. Geometry statistics occupy an optional four-address tail after the ordinary per-draw fields; shaders compiled without statistics omit its loads and uploads. Active GS or clipping queries add four 32-bit scratch counters to the existing stream scratch, and null statistic addresses skip the Poly writes independently of XFB. The stream parameter layout is unchanged by statistics gating. Packed report offsets are computed once when the pool is created. Counting uses the existing queue, compiler, descriptor and query-copy machinery, with no CPU readback during recording or separate GPU queue.\n\nThe adaptation reuses Mesa MIT-licensed Honeykrisp and Poly source, retaining attribution in the owned sources. Timestamps, visibility samples and fabricated zero substitutions do not replace pipeline statistics.\n"));
